=begin pod

=TITLE Liblevenshtein

=SUBTITLE Fast edit distances and snapshot-consistent fuzzy search

=head1 Synopsis

    use Liblevenshtein;
    say distance('kitten', 'sitting');

=head1 Search resources

C<Transducer.new(:dictionary($dictionary), :algorithm(STANDARD))> retains a
versioned Vinary Tree dictionary provider. C<query> returns an exclusive
C<QueryCursor> over the immutable revision captured at query start. Iterate
the cursor for owned C<Match> values or call C<reduce-batches> for bounded
batch processing.

=head1 Bounded repeated-query caching

C<QueryCache.new(:$transducer, :max-entries(1024),
:max-weight(64 * 1024 * 1024))> retains the transducer and memoizes complete
exact results. C<.query> accepts C<Str>, C<Blob>, and positional u64-token
inputs. C<.stats> reports requests, hits, misses, admissions, rejections,
evictions, resident entries, and logical weight; C<.clear> drops residency,
while C<.reset-stats> preserves residency and resets counters.

The native policy uses TinyLFU approximate-frequency admission and SIEVE
victim selection. Approximation affects only residency: a miss always computes
and returns the exact result. The cache has hard bounds and no internal lock.
It is exclusive, so parallel programs create one cache per worker. A miss
captures one immutable revision before materializing results; a provider that
does not publish stable snapshot identity is rejected rather than allowed to
serve stale matches.

    my $cache = QueryCache.new(:$transducer, :max-entries(512));
    LEAVE $cache.close;
    my @matches = $cache.query('speling', 2).list;
    say $cache.stats.hits;

=head1 Ownership

Call C<close> on transducers, query caches, cursors, phonetic patterns, and
rewrite-rule sets, and standalone automata and online states. C<DESTROY> is a leak fallback. Each cache and cursor is
single-consumer, while
independent cursors and immutable transducers may run concurrently.

=head1 Standalone generalized and universal automata

C<GeneralizedAutomaton.new($maximum-distance, $operation-set)> accepts a
C<GeneralizedOperationSet> or positional collection of
C<GeneralizedOperation> descriptors. Each descriptor names the source and
target Unicode-scalar arity, cost, diagnostic name, and applicability. A
C<GeneralizedRestriction> or C<source =E<gt> target> pair restricts a
C<APPLICABILITY-LISTED> operation directionally. Native construction owns a
validated copy. C<evaluate($source, $target)> returns a
C<GeneralizedObservation> with a rational C<distance> when accepted, and
C<Nil> otherwise. C<online($source)> returns an exclusive state with
C<advance($scalar)> and C<observation>; an empty current row can recover via
a later multi-unit operation.

    my $rules = GeneralizedOperationSet.new(operations => [
        GeneralizedOperation.new(
            consume-source => 2, consume-target => 1, weight => 0.5,
            name => 'ph to f', applicability => APPLICABILITY-LISTED,
            restrictions => ['ph' => 'f'],
        ),
    ]);
    my $generalized = GeneralizedAutomaton.new(1, $rules);
    LEAVE $generalized.close;
    say $generalized.evaluate('ph', 'f').distance; # 1/2

C<UniversalAutomaton.new($maximum-distance, :variant(...))> selects standard,
adjacent-transposition, or merge-and-split native position automata. With no
policy it uses the unrestricted native specialization for C<Str>, C<Blob>,
and unsigned-u64 C<Positional> inputs. C<UniversalPolicy.new(:domain(...),
:equivalences([...]))> adds nonempty directional zero-cost substitution pairs
and fixes the unit domain. Its C<UniversalObservation> reports consumed target
units, source units, liveness, and acceptance. The frontier cannot recover
after C<alive> becomes false. C<online($source)> advances one scalar string,
byte integer, or u64 integer per call. Mixed domains and out-of-range units
throw instead of coercing.

    my $policy = UniversalPolicy.new(
        domain => UNICODE-SCALAR, equivalences => ['p' => 'f'],
    );
    my $universal = UniversalAutomaton.new(0, :$policy);
    LEAVE $universal.close;
    say $universal.accepts('p', 'f'); # True
    say $universal.accepts('f', 'p'); # False

C<AutomatonLimits.new> configures source, target, retained-cell, and per-step
work ceilings. Omit C<:limits> for the native defaults. A rejected online
advance does not mutate the committed prefix; use C<observation> to inspect
it. Online states own their native configuration after their parent closes.

=head1 API

=item C<distance>, C<damerau-distance>, C<true-damerau-distance>, C<merge-and-split-distance>

Compute standalone distances over two C<Str> values (Unicode scalars), two
C<Blob> values (raw bytes), or two C<Positional> values (full-range unsigned
64-bit tokens). C<damerau-distance> is optimal string alignment, which is
not a metric; C<true-damerau-distance> is unrestricted Damerau-Levenshtein.
C<merge-and-split-distance> also admits symmetric one-to-two edits.

=item C<hamming-distance>, C<indel-distance>

Hamming counts unequal positions and returns C<Nil> for unequal lengths.
Indel permits insertion/deletion but not substitution. Both support the same
three unit domains as C<distance>.

=item C<affine-gap-distance>, C<AffineGapCosts>

Pass an C<AffineGapCosts> object with nonnegative C<gap-open>, C<gap-extend>,
and C<substitution> integer costs. A gap of length C<k> costs
C<gap-open + k * gap-extend>. The exact algorithm is the native Gotoh
implementation; an unrepresentable/overflow result returns C<Nil>. The
thresholded form computes the exact score then applies the inclusive bound.

All distance functions accept C<:threshold($bound)>. A score above the
inclusive bound returns C<Nil>; native undefined and above-bound cases have
different sentinels, but the Raku optional result intentionally maps both to
C<Nil>. Invalid inputs throw. Standard distance dispatches internally to
the applicable Myers/ASCII or runtime SIMD kernel; there is no public kernel
selection knob because those kernels preserve the same metric semantics.

    my $costs = AffineGapCosts.new(
        gap-open => 3, gap-extend => 2, substitution => 10,
    );
    say affine-gap-distance('a', 'abcd', $costs); # 9
    say hamming-distance([0, 2**64 - 1], [0, 0]); # 1

=item C<Transducer>, C<QueryCursor>, C<Match>

Configure an algorithm over a retained provider and traverse owned results in
bounded batches.

=item C<QueryCache>, C<QueryCacheStats>

Run repeated complete queries through the bounded native TinyLFU/SIEVE cache,
inspect policy counters, and clear residency independently from counters.

=item C<STANDARD>, C<TRANSPOSITION>, C<MERGE-AND-SPLIT>, C<DAMERAU-LEVENSHTEIN>

Select standard Levenshtein, optimal string alignment, symmetric merge/split,
or unrestricted Damerau-Levenshtein semantics. Standard, merge/split, and
unrestricted Damerau are metrics; optimal string alignment is not.

=item C<PhoneticPattern>, C<PhoneticRuleSet>

Compile reusable phonetic languages and rewrite rules when the native
phonetic feature is present.

=head1 Errors

Native failures throw C<X::Liblevenshtein> with a stable numeric status, the
failing operation, and a copied diagnostic.

=end pod
